{
  "id": "central-pattern-generator",
  "title": "Central Pattern Generator",
  "tags": [
    "rhythm",
    "half-centre oscillator",
    "adaptation",
    "locomotion"
  ],
  "references": [
    "Brown (1911) The intrinsic factors in the act of progression in the mammal. Proc. R. Soc. B.",
    "Marder & Bucher (2001) Central pattern generators and the control of rhythmic movements. Curr. Biol."
  ],
  "network": {
    "version": 1,
    "name": "Central Pattern Generator",
    "description": "Two tonically driven populations inhibit each other (a half-centre oscillator). Whichever side is active suppresses the other until spike-frequency adaptation tires it out; the other side is then released and takes over. The result is a steady left/right (flexor/extensor) rhythm with no rhythmic input at all.",
    "biology": "Central pattern generators in the spinal cord and brainstem produce walking, swimming, breathing and chewing rhythms even when sensory feedback is removed (Graham Brown's half-centre model, 1911). Mutual inhibition plus a slow fatigue process (here adaptation currents, in biology e.g. Ca²⁺-activated K⁺ channels) is the classic mechanism.",
